{"results":[{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"P37173","uniProtkbId":"TGFR2_HUMAN","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"TGF-beta receptor type-2"},"shortNames":[{"value":"TGFR-2"}],"ecNumbers":[{"value":"2.7.11.30"}]},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"value":"TGF-beta type II receptor"}},{"fullName":{"value":"Transforming growth factor-beta receptor type II"},"shortNames":[{"value":"TGF-beta receptor type II"},{"value":"TbetaR-II"}]}],"flag":"Precursor"},"genes":[{"geneName":{"value":"TGFBR2"}}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"7774578"}],"value":"Transmembrane serine/threonine kinase forming with the TGF-beta type I serine/threonine kinase receptor, TGFBR1, the non-promiscuous receptor for the TGF-beta cytokines TGFB1, TGFB2 and TGFB3. Transduces the TGFB1, TGFB2 and TGFB3 signal from the cell surface to the cytoplasm and thus regulates a plethora of physiological and pathological processes including cell cycle arrest in epithelial and hematopoietic cells, control of mesenchymal cell proliferation and differentiation, wound healing, extracellular matrix production, immunosuppression and carcinogenesis. The formation of the receptor complex composed of 2 TGFBR1 and 2 TGFBR2 molecules symmetrically bound to the cytokine dimer results in the phosphorylation and activation of TGFBR1 by the constitutively active TGFBR2. Activated TGFBR1 phosphorylates SMAD2 which dissociates from the receptor and interacts with SMAD4. The SMAD2-SMAD4 complex is subsequently translocated to the nucleus where it modulates the transcription of the TGF-beta-regulated genes. This constitutes the canonical SMAD-dependent TGF-beta signaling cascade. Also involved in non-canonical, SMAD-independent TGF-beta signaling pathways"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8635485"}],"value":"Has transforming growth factor beta-activated receptor activity"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION","molecule":"Isoform 1"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8635485"}],"value":"Has transforming growth factor beta-activated receptor activity"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION","molecule":"Isoform 2"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"34568316"}],"value":"Binds TGFB1, TGFB2 and TGFB3 in the picomolar affinity range without the participation of additional receptors. Blocks activation of SMAD2 and SMAD3 by TGFB1"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION","molecule":"Isoform 3"}],"extraAttributes":{"uniParcId":"UPI000011DD7E"}},
{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"P01137","uniProtkbId":"TGFB1_HUMAN","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"Transforming growth factor beta-1 proprotein"}},"contains":[{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"2982829"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"3162913"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"7737999"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"8471846"}],"value":"Latency-associated peptide"},"shortNames":[{"value":"LAP"}]}},{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"2982829"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"3162913"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"7737999"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"8471846"}],"value":"Transforming growth factor beta-1"},"shortNames":[{"value":"TGF-beta-1"}]}}],"flag":"Precursor"},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000312","source":"HGNC","id":"HGNC:11766"}],"value":"TGFB1"},"synonyms":[{"value":"TGFB"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"29109152"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"27252363"}],"value":"Transforming growth factor beta-1 proprotein: Precursor of the Latency-associated peptide (LAP) and Transforming growth factor beta-1 (TGF-beta-1) chains, which constitute the regulatory and active subunit of TGF-beta-1, respectively"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19651619"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19750484"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"2022183"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"22278742"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"28117447"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8617200"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8939931"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"29909984"}],"value":"Required to maintain the Transforming growth factor beta-1 (TGF-beta-1) chain in a latent state during storage in extracellular matrix (PubMed:28117447). Associates non-covalently with TGF-beta-1 and regulates its activation via interaction with 'milieu molecules', such as LTBP1, LRRC32/GARP and LRRC33/NRROS, that control activation of TGF-beta-1 (PubMed:19651619, PubMed:19750484, PubMed:2022183, PubMed:22278742, PubMed:8617200, PubMed:8939931). Interaction with LRRC33/NRROS regulates activation of TGF-beta-1 in macrophages and microglia (Probable). Interaction with LRRC32/GARP controls activation of TGF-beta-1 on the surface of activated regulatory T-cells (Tregs) (PubMed:19651619, PubMed:19750484, PubMed:22278742). Interaction with integrins (ITGAV:ITGB6 or ITGAV:ITGB8) results in distortion of the Latency-associated peptide chain and subsequent release of the active TGF-beta-1 (PubMed:22278742, PubMed:28117447)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION","molecule":"Latency-associated peptide"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source":"UniProtKB","id":"P04202"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19651619"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19750484"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"20207738"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"2022183"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"22278742"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"25310401"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"25893292"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"28117447"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"29109152"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"29483653"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"30696809"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8617200"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8939931"}],"value":"Multifunctional protein that regulates the growth and differentiation of various cell types and is involved in various processes, such as normal development, immune function, microglia function and responses to neurodegeneration (By similarity). Activation into mature form follows different steps: following cleavage of the proprotein in the Golgi apparatus, Latency-associated peptide (LAP) and Transforming growth factor beta-1 (TGF-beta-1) chains remain non-covalently linked rendering TGF-beta-1 inactive during storage in extracellular matrix (PubMed:29109152). At the same time, LAP chain interacts with 'milieu molecules', such as LTBP1, LRRC32/GARP and LRRC33/NRROS that control activation of TGF-beta-1 and maintain it in a latent state during storage in extracellular milieus (PubMed:19651619, PubMed:19750484, PubMed:2022183, PubMed:22278742, PubMed:8617200, PubMed:8939931). TGF-beta-1 is released from LAP by integrins (ITGAV:ITGB6 or ITGAV:ITGB8): integrin-binding to LAP stabilizes an alternative conformation of the LAP bowtie tail and results in distortion of the LAP chain and subsequent release of the active TGF-beta-1 (PubMed:22278742, PubMed:28117447). Once activated following release of LAP, TGF-beta-1 acts by binding to TGF-beta receptors (TGFBR1 and TGFBR2), which transduce signal (PubMed:20207738). While expressed by many cells types, TGF-beta-1 only has a very localized range of action within cell environment thanks to fine regulation of its activation by Latency-associated peptide chain (LAP) and 'milieu molecules' (By similarity). Plays an important role in bone remodeling: acts as a potent stimulator of osteoblastic bone formation, causing chemotaxis, proliferation and differentiation in committed osteoblasts (By similarity). Can promote either T-helper 17 cells (Th17) or regulatory T-cells (Treg) lineage differentiation in a concentration-dependent manner (By similarity). At high concentrations, leads to FOXP3-mediated suppression of RORC and down-regulation of IL-17 expression, favoring Treg cell development (By similarity). At low concentrations in concert with IL-6 and IL-21, leads to expression of the IL-17 and IL-23 receptors, favoring differentiation to Th17 cells (By similarity). Stimulates sustained production of collagen through the activation of CREB3L1 by regulated intramembrane proteolysis (RIP) (PubMed:25310401). Mediates SMAD2/3 activation by inducing its phosphorylation and subsequent translocation to the nucleus (PubMed:25893292, PubMed:29483653, PubMed:30696809). Positively regulates odontoblastic differentiation in dental papilla cells, via promotion of IPO7-mediated translocation of phosphorylated SMAD2 to the nucleus and subsequent transcription of target genes (By similarity). Can induce epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT) and cell migration in various cell types (PubMed:25893292, PubMed:30696809)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION","molecule":"Transforming growth factor beta-1"}],"extraAttributes":{"uniParcId":"UPI000013C7E1"}},
{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"P36897","uniProtkbId":"TGFR1_HUMAN","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"TGF-beta receptor type-1"},"shortNames":[{"value":"TGFR-1"}],"ecNumbers":[{"value":"2.7.11.30"}]},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"value":"Activin A receptor type II-like protein kinase of 53kD"}},{"fullName":{"value":"Activin receptor-like kinase 5"},"shortNames":[{"value":"ALK-5"},{"value":"ALK5"}]},{"fullName":{"value":"Serine/threonine-protein kinase receptor R4"},"shortNames":[{"value":"SKR4"}]},{"fullName":{"value":"TGF-beta type I receptor"}},{"fullName":{"value":"Transforming growth factor-beta receptor type I"},"shortNames":[{"value":"TGF-beta receptor type I"},{"value":"TbetaR-I"}]}],"flag":"Precursor"},"genes":[{"geneName":{"value":"TGFBR1"},"synonyms":[{"value":"ALK5"},{"value":"SKR4"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15761148"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"16754747"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"18758450"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"33914044"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"7774578"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8752209"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8980228"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9346908"}],"value":"Transmembrane serine/threonine kinase forming with the TGF-beta type II serine/threonine kinase receptor, TGFBR2, the non-promiscuous receptor for the TGF-beta cytokines TGFB1, TGFB2 and TGFB3. Transduces the TGFB1, TGFB2 and TGFB3 signal from the cell surface to the cytoplasm and is thus regulating a plethora of physiological and pathological processes including cell cycle arrest in epithelial and hematopoietic cells, control of mesenchymal cell proliferation and differentiation, wound healing, extracellular matrix production, immunosuppression and carcinogenesis (PubMed:33914044). The formation of the receptor complex composed of 2 TGFBR1 and 2 TGFBR2 molecules symmetrically bound to the cytokine dimer results in the phosphorylation and the activation of TGFBR1 by the constitutively active TGFBR2. Activated TGFBR1 phosphorylates SMAD2 which dissociates from the receptor and interacts with SMAD4. The SMAD2-SMAD4 complex is subsequently translocated to the nucleus where it modulates the transcription of the TGF-beta-regulated genes. This constitutes the canonical SMAD-dependent TGF-beta signaling cascade. Also involved in non-canonical, SMAD-independent TGF-beta signaling pathways. For instance, TGFBR1 induces TRAF6 autoubiquitination which in turn results in MAP3K7 ubiquitination and activation to trigger apoptosis. Also regulates epithelial to mesenchymal transition through a SMAD-independent signaling pathway through PARD6A phosphorylation and activation"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"}],"extraAttributes":{"uniParcId":"UPI000011D62A"}},
{"entryType":"UniProtKB unreviewed (TrEMBL)","primaryAccession":"A0A499FJK2","uniProtkbId":"A0A499FJK2_HUMAN","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"PIRNR","id":"PIRNR001787"}],"value":"Transforming growth factor beta"}}},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000313","source":"Ensembl","id":"ENSP00000221930.4"}],"value":"TGFB1"}}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"ARBA","id":"ARBA00003201"}],"value":"Required to maintain the Transforming growth factor beta-1 (TGF-beta-1) chain in a latent state during storage in extracellular matrix. Associates non-covalently with TGF-beta-1 and regulates its activation via interaction with 'milieu molecules', such as LTBP1, LRRC32/GARP and LRRC33/NRROS, that control activation of TGF-beta-1. Interaction with LRRC33/NRROS regulates activation of TGF-beta-1 in macrophages and microglia. Interaction with LRRC32/GARP controls activation of TGF-beta-1 on the surface of activated regulatory T-cells (Tregs). Interaction with integrins (ITGAV:ITGB6 or ITGAV:ITGB8) results in distortion of the Latency-associated peptide chain and subsequent release of the active TGF-beta-1"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"ARBA","id":"ARBA00002007"}],"value":"Transforming growth factor beta-1 proprotein: Precursor of the Latency-associated peptide (LAP) and Transforming growth factor beta-1 (TGF-beta-1) chains, which constitute the regulatory and active subunit of TGF-beta-1, respectively"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"}],"extraAttributes":{"uniParcId":"UPI000013C7E1"}},
{"entryType":"UniProtKB unreviewed (TrEMBL)","primaryAccession":"A0AAQ5BI06","uniProtkbId":"A0AAQ5BI06_HUMAN","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"RuleBase","id":"RU361271"}],"value":"Serine/threonine-protein kinase receptor"},"ecNumbers":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"RuleBase","id":"RU361271"}],"value":"2.7.11.30"}]}},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000313","source":"Ensembl","id":"ENSP00000519656.1"}],"value":"TGFBR2"}}],"comments":[],"extraAttributes":{"uniParcId":"UPI0000412184"}},
{"entryType":"UniProtKB unreviewed (TrEMBL)","primaryAccession":"A0AAQ5BI03","uniProtkbId":"A0AAQ5BI03_HUMAN","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"ARBA","id":"ARBA00017567"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"PIRNR","id":"PIRNR037393"}],"value":"TGF-beta receptor type-2"},"shortNames":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"PIRNR","id":"PIRNR037393"}],"value":"TGFR-2"}],"ecNumbers":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"ARBA","id":"ARBA00012401"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"PIRNR","id":"PIRNR037393"}],"value":"2.7.11.30"}]},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"ARBA","id":"ARBA00032053"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"PIRNR","id":"PIRNR037393"}],"value":"TGF-beta type II receptor"}},{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"ARBA","id":"ARBA00033122"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"PIRNR","id":"PIRNR037393"}],"value":"Transforming growth factor-beta receptor type II"}}]},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000313","source":"Ensembl","id":"ENSP00000519658.1"}],"value":"TGFBR2"}}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"PIRNR","id":"PIRNR037393"}],"value":"Transmembrane serine/threonine kinase forming with the TGF-beta type I serine/threonine kinase receptor, TGFBR1, the non-promiscuous receptor for the TGF-beta cytokines TGFB1, TGFB2 and TGFB3. Transduces the TGFB1, TGFB2 and TGFB3 signal from the cell surface to the cytoplasm and is thus regulating a plethora of physiological and pathological processes including cell cycle arrest in epithelial and hematopoietic cells, control of mesenchymal cell proliferation and differentiation, wound healing, extracellular matrix production, immunosuppression and carcinogenesis. The formation of the receptor complex composed of 2 TGFBR1 and 2 TGFBR2 molecules symmetrically bound to the cytokine dimer results in the phosphorylation and the activation of TGFRB1 by the constitutively active TGFBR2. Activated TGFBR1 phosphorylates SMAD2 which dissociates from the receptor and interacts with SMAD4. The SMAD2-SMAD4 complex is subsequently translocated to the nucleus where it modulates the transcription of the TGF-beta-regulated genes. This constitutes the canonical SMAD-dependent TGF-beta signaling cascade. Also involved in non-canonical, SMAD-independent TGF-beta signaling pathways"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"}],"extraAttributes":{"uniParcId":"UPI0032D056CD"}},
